# Cultural Landscapes Inventory: Pickett's Charge Battlefield (National Park Service, 2024)
# Read: PDF pp. 41-51 (chronology, physical history to July 1863), 72-73 (land use), 78-85 (vegetation),
#       95-99 (buildings and structures), 105, 115-117 (small-scale features, fences).
@ NPS-CLI-PCB

# --- 1863 character of the landscape (physical history) ---
49 | general | Gettysburg region before the battle | - | pre-1863 to 1863-07 | stated | Before the battle wood fences and stone walls defined property lines and separated fields from pastures, woodlots and public roads | Prior to the battle, the region surrounding Gettysburg >>> woodlots, and public roads.
49 | fences | Pitzer, McMillan and Bliss farms (northern part of the Pickett's Charge field) | FEN-mcmillan,bld-bliss-farm,bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 the fields of the Pitzer, McMillan and Bliss farms were smaller to the north and larger to the south and were delineated by a mixture of worm and post and rail fencing | The 1863 character of the Pitzer-McMillan-Bliss farms >>> worm and post and rail fencing.
49 | orchards | McMillan farm orchards and nursery | bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 large orchards, including a commercial orchard nursery at the McMillan farm, and woodlots interrupted the open fields of the Pitzer, McMillan and Bliss farms | Large orchards, including a commercial orchard nursery >>> open expanse of the fields. | #s11
49 | fences | Long Lane and the McMillan-McDannell farm lane | - | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 Long Lane and the McMillan-McDannell farm lane were lined with post and rail fencing | Long Lane extended southwest from the Borough >>> lined with post and rail fencing.
49 | woodlots | Stevens Run | - | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 Stevens Run had sparse vegetation along its banks | Stevens Run flowed through the eastern section of the area with sparse vegetation along its banks.
49 | fences | Codori, Henry Spangler, Staub and Sherfy farms (southern part of the Pickett's Charge field) | bld-codori-farm,bld-sherfy-farm,FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 the Codori, H. Spangler, Staub and Sherfy farms had medium to large fields primarily defined by worm and post and rail fencing | The 1863 character of the Codori-H. Spangler-Staub-Sherfy farms >>> defined by worm and post and rail fencing.
49 | orchards | Orchards around the Emmitsburg, Wheatfield and Fairfield roads junction | bld-sherfy-farm,FEN-sherfy-orchard | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 seven orchards and fruit gardens stood among the crop fields and meadows, concentrated around the junction of the Emmitsburg, Wheatfield and Fairfield roads | Seven orchards and fruit gardens punctuated >>> Wheatfield, and Fairfield Roads.
49 | woodlots | Seminary Ridge south end and Warfield Ridge north end | - | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 woodlots marked the southern end of Seminary Ridge and the northern end of Warfield Ridge | Woodlots marked the southern end of Seminary Ridge and northern end of Warfield Ridge.
49 | fences | Emmitsburg Road and Fairfield (Millerstown) Road, Codori to Sherfy | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 the Emmitsburg Road, extending southwest from the southern end of the Borough, and the Fairfield Road (presently Millerstown Road) were lined predominantly by worm and post and rail fencing | #s22-23
50 | fences | Farmsteads and fence lines on the field of Pickett's Charge | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07 | stated | The open fields between Seminary Ridge and Cemetery Ridge were used in two Confederate attacks (July 2 and July 3); the field's lack of cover, together with the numerous farmsteads and fence lines that helped break up the Southern formations, resulted in the repulse of both assaults | #s23-25
51 | fences | Emmitsburg Road parallel fences during Pickett's Charge (Col. Rawley W. Martin, as quoted by the inventory from Campbell 2004) | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | A participant (Col. Rawley W. Martin) recalled that the parallel fences at the Emmitsburg Road impeded the advance, that men crowded at openings where fences had been thrown down, and that men halted to climb the fences | Col. Rawley W. Martin recalled these >>> halt in order to climb the fences.”
51 | fences | Fences crossed by Davis's brigade, July 3 (Brig. Gen. Joseph Davis, as quoted by the inventory from Campbell 2004) | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-03 | stated | Davis reported that fences interrupted the advance and deranged the alignment | Brig. Gen. Joseph Davis reported, “Our advance >>> more or less deranged.”
51 | orchards | William Bliss orchard | bld-bliss-farm | 1863-07 | stated | On July 2 and 3 the Bliss orchard gave the only substantial cover for skirmishers on the nearly 1,200 yards of open ground between Cemetery Ridge and Seminary Ridge | During the fighting on July 2 and 3, the W. Bliss orchard >>> Cemetery Ridge and Seminary Ridge.
51 | roads | Lane through the Emanuel Pitzer / Henry Spangler woodlot | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | Pickett's Division used the section of lane through the Pitzer / Spangler woodlot as its approach on the morning of July 3 | The section of the lane that passed through >>> attack later that day.
51 | damage | Battlefield after the armies left | - | 1863-07 (after the battle) | stated | The armies left thrown-down farm fencing, trampled crops, stone walls and fencing rearranged into breastworks, splintered woodlands and orchards, and dead livestock | They also left behind the kinds of consummate destruction >>> dead livestock.

# --- Farmsteads (land use) ---
72 | fences | David McMillan farm fences | FEN-mcmillan,bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | On the morning of July 1 Union troops advancing across McMillan's land tore down his fences to ease the movement of troops | On the morning of July 1, 1863 Union troops advanced across McMillan’s land, tearing down his fences to facilitate movement of troops.
72 | fields | David McMillan farm corn crop | bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | A Union battery placed for a short time in the McMillan yard damaged McMillan's corn crop | For a short time a Union artillery battery was placed in the yard, >>> damaged McMillan's corn crop.
72 | fences | David McMillan farm fencing used for entrenchments | FEN-mcmillan,bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07 (Confederate occupation after July 1) | stated | During the Confederate occupation McMillan's fencing was used to build entrenchments and his crops and fruit trees were destroyed | During this time, McMillan’s fencing was used in constructing entrenchments, and his crops and fruit trees were destroyed.
72 | buildings | David McMillan farm buildings | bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 the McMillan property had three buildings: the house, the barn and a log building of about 18 by 20 feet; only the house stands today | There were three buildings on the McMillan property in 1863, >>> The only extant building is the house.
72 | buildings | David McMillan house, battle damage | bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The McMillan house was damaged by artillery shells and gutted of its contents by the occupying Confederates | The McMillan house was damaged by artillery shells and completely gutted of its contents by the Confederates occupying the farm.
72 | buildings | Nicholas Codori farmstead today | bld-codori-farm | modern 2024 (not the 1863 building group) | stated | Today the Nicholas Codori farmstead includes a house, barn, wagon shed/corn crib and garage/chicken house arranged around a gravel area | Today the farmstead includes a house, barn, wagon shed/corn crib, garage/chicken house arranged around a gravel area. | #s13
73 | buildings | Henry Spangler barn | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Henry Spangler barn was burned during the battle | During the battle the barn was burned. | #s6
73 | hospitals | Henry Spangler farm (Emmitsburg Road) | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The Henry Spangler farm may have served as a temporary hospital | The farm may have served as a temporary hospital. | #s6
73 | hospitals | Shultz house (Seminary Ridge, former Oak Ridge Academy building) | - | 1863-07 | stated | During and after the battle the Shultz house was used for emergency aid and as a temporary hospital | During and after the battle, the house was used for emergency aid and temporary hospital purposes. | #s20
73 | buildings | Shultz house | - | 1837 building; altered 1890s | stated | The Shultz house (inventory dates 1837, 1890s) is a 2.5-story brick building that was originally a Greek Revival house designed to house academy students on the upper floor during the pre-war years; in the late 1890s the roof was raised and the style changed to Queen Anne by a York architect (Dempwolf) | #s21-23 | #s20
73 | buildings | Shultz house, battle damage | - | 1863-07 | hedged | Return fire from Union batteries was reported to have caused some damage to the Shultz house | Return fire from those Union batteries was reported to have caused some damage to the house. | #s20

# --- Fields, orchards and woodlots (vegetation) ---
78 | fields | Barrett Field (outlot, east slope of Seminary Ridge) | - | 1863-07 | stated | Barrett Field was in corn at the time of the battle | This small field was in corn at the time of the battle and was not associated with any particular farm. | #s5
78 | fields | Jacob Benner Corn Field and the crop fields on the Emmitsburg Road | FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07 | stated | The Jacob Benner field was one of three crop fields abutting the Emmitsburg Road; north-south fencing separated these fields from the Bliss farm | It is one of three crop fields >>> provided cover for these skirmishers. | #s14
78 | fields | Bliss farm fields | bld-bliss-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Bliss farm had fence lines, open crop fields and meadows, the scene of skirmishing from the night of July 1 until July 5 | Incessant skirmish firing and deadly sharpshooting >>> retreated on July 5.
78 | buildings | Bliss farm buildings | bld-bliss-farm | 1863-07 (destroyed) | stated | The Bliss buildings were destroyed as a result of the battle and the fields were later absorbed into the Codori farm | Because the buildings were destroyed as a result of the battle, the farm fields were eventually absorbed into the Codori farming operations. | #s25
