80 | orchards | McMillan orchard and nursery (replanted) | bld-mcmillan-farm | 2009 replanting (modern) | stated | Non-historic woods were removed in 2007 and apples were planted in 2009 in the McMillan Orchard and Nursery | #s11-12
80 | woodlots | McMillan Woods | - | 1863-07 | stated | McMillan Woods was a deciduous woodlot that gave cover to Confederate infantry supporting the artillery on the crest of the ridge | This deciduous woodlot provided cover for infantry of the Confederate army as it lay in support of Confederate artillery on the crest of the ridge.
80 | woodlots | McMillan Woods, ownership and later change | - | 1863 ownership; northern third cut before park ownership, reforested 1960s | stated | The northern third (Horting) was cut down before National Park Service ownership and reforested in the 1960s as successional forest, mainly cedar; the southern McMillan part keeps its historic limits and is still deciduous | The northern one-third of the woods served as the woodlot >>> is still composed of deciduous trees.
80 | earthworks | McMillan Woods entrenchments | - | 1863-07-03 (after the repulse) | stated | After the repulse of Pickett's Charge Confederate infantrymen retreated to McMillan Woods and constructed entrenchments for a defensive line in expectation of a Union counterattack | #s20-23
81 | woodlots | Samuel Pitzer Woods | - | ~part cut before park acquisition; replanted about 1904 | stated | Before acquisition by the park the farmer cut down some of the woods (S. Pitzer Woods), but about 1904 the Gettysburg National Park Commission replanted missing portions | Before acquisition by the park, the farmer cut down some of these woods but about 1904 the GNP Commission replanted missing portions. | #s9
81 | woodlots | Samuel Pitzer Woods | - | 1863-07 | stated | Pitzer Woods was a deciduous woodlot on the northern end of Warfield Ridge, used for cover and concealment by Confederate forces on July 2 and 3 | #s9-12
81 | earthworks | Samuel Pitzer Woods breastworks | - | 1863-07-04 | stated | On July 4 Confederate troops built breastworks through Pitzer Woods | On July 4, Confederate troops re-established defensive positions within the woods and constructed breastworks through them. | #s9
