# --- Buildings and structures ---
95 | buildings | Christian Shefferer farmhouse (Warfield Ridge) | - | 1863-07; replaced 1874 | stated | The house at 175 Millerstown Road (the William J. Althoff farm house, dated 1874 in the inventory) occupies the foundation of the 1863 Christian Shefferer farmhouse | #s8-9
95 | earthworks | Anderson's Division breastworks (stone wall near Pitzer's Woods) | - | modern survey of a feature dated 1863 | stated | A stone wall 420 feet long, 1-1.5 feet wide and up to 3 feet high generally matches the breastworks shown on the park's 1863 Existing Condition map No.12 and labeled 'Anderson's Division Breastworks' | Stone wall 420 feet long, 1-1.5 feet wide, and up to 3 feet high. >>> “Anderson’s Division Breastworks.”
95 | fences | Barrett Field north boundary stone wall with rider fence | - | modern survey; shown on the park 1863 existing-condition map | stated | A stone wall with rider fence on the north boundary of Barrett Field (315 feet as surveyed today, parts missing) matches the stone wall and rider fence on a park 1863 existing-condition map; the dimensions are the present ones | Stone wall and rider fence 315 feet long, >>> Fence and Rider Fence.”
96 | buildings | Nicholas Codori house | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 (1834 section); additions 1877 and 1941 | stated | The Codori house (inventory dates c.1834 and 1877) is a two-story brick L-shaped building on a granite foundation: the 1834 section measures 18x33 feet, the 1877 portion is a cross-gable ell addition of 25x15 feet, and an 1941 frame addition in the crook of the ell measures 10x24 feet | #s2-5 | Nicholas Codori Farm: House.
96 | hospitals | Nicholas Codori house (shelter for wounded) | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07-02 to 07-03 | stated | On July 2 two regiments of Harrow's brigade moved forward to the road beside the Codori house and were overrun, and wounded sought shelter within the house; on July 3 the house was an obstacle to Garnett's Virginia brigade in the Pickett-Pettigrew Assault, and Confederate wounded joined the Union wounded in the house until the conclusion of the battle | #s8-11 | #s1
96 | buildings | Nicholas Codori barn | bld-codori-farm | 1884 (post-battle building) | stated | The present Codori barn (inventory date 1884, contributing) is a two-storey Pennsylvania bank barn of 43 by 80 feet; the report calls it a post-war building | #s17-20 | #s16
96 | buildings | Nicholas Codori wagon shed and corn crib | bld-codori-farm | 1872 (post-battle building); moved 1943 | stated | The Codori wagon shed and corn crib (inventory date 1872, contributing) is a one-storey frame building of 41 by 31 feet with a loft and a stone pier foundation; in 1943 it was rolled back about 40 feet from the road to a new foundation | #s22-23 | #s21
97 | fences | Stone wall on Seminary Ridge east of Confederate Avenue (Pegram's Battalion stone wall) | - | ~pre-1863 origin; dimensions as surveyed today (modern) | stated | Pegram's Battalion Stone Wall (inventory date pre-1863, contributing) is a dry-laid wall of irregular granite and ironstone boulders, 2,400 feet long and averaging 3.5 feet wide and 3 feet high, running along the east side of Confederate Avenue from the vicinity of the McMillan House; the stone wall was erected as a farm field boundary and then incorporated by the armies into battle positions | #s4-9
97 | fences | Stone wall on Seminary Ridge east of Confederate Avenue (Pegram's Battalion stone wall), origin | - | pre-1863 | stated | The wall was erected as a farm field boundary and then incorporated by the armies into battle positions | The north terminus is at vicinity of McMillan House >>> incorporated by the armies into battle positions.
97 | fences | Stone wall on the east edge of Shultz's Woods (Pender's Division stone wall) | - | ~pre-1863 origin; dimensions as surveyed today (modern) | stated | Pender's Division Stone Wall (inventory date pre-1863, contributing), a dry-laid wall of irregular-sized granite and ironstone boulders along the eastern edge of Shultz's Woods from Fairfield Road/Middle Street to opposite the former Armory, is 1,348 feet long, 3 feet wide and 3 feet high; it was erected as a farm field boundary and then incorporated by the armies into battle positions | #s10-14
97 | fences | Stone wall on the north edge of Pitzer Woods | - | ~pre-1863 origin; dimensions as surveyed today (modern) | stated | Pitzer's Woods Stone Wall (inventory date pre-1863, contributing) is a dry-laid wall of irregular-shaped granite boulders, 1,300 feet long, 2 feet wide and 2 feet high, on the northern edge of Pitzer Woods on Seminary Ridge near Spangler Run; it was erected as a farm field boundary and then incorporated by the armies into battle positions, and its location matches the rider fence depicted on 1863 Existing Condition map No. 12 | #s15-21
97 | fences | Stone wall on the north edge of Pitzer Woods, rider fence | - | 1863-07 (per the park 1863 existing-condition map) | stated | The location of the Pitzer's Woods Stone Wall matches the rider fence depicted on 1863 Existing Condition map No. 12 by Kathy Harrison | The location matches the rider fence depicted on 1863 Existing Condition map No. 12 by Kathy Harrison. | #s15
97 | buildings | Joseph Sherfy house | bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07 (two-storey brick house with one-storey kitchen ell); additions after 1870 and in the 1880s | stated | The Sherfy house (inventory date 1840-1860, contributing) is a 2-story, 3-bay brick Georgian style building; the original house had a one-story brick kitchen ell on the back, to which a brick second floor was added after 1870 and a wood frame addition in the 1880s | #s22-25
97 | buildings | Joseph Sherfy house, battle damage | bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The Sherfy house, central to the battlefields of July 2 and 3, was incorporated on the second day of the battle into the battle line of Graham's Union Brigade; men of one regiment fortified themselves inside and fired from the east side before the position was outflanked and overrun, and the fighting damaged the exterior and interior of the house, the damage still visible today | #s28-32 | #s22
97 | buildings | Joseph Sherfy barn | bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07 (English barn, burned); present barn 1868 | stated | The present Sherfy barn (inventory date 1868) is a two-storey frame Pennsylvania bank barn built on the site of the battle-era barn, which was destroyed during the battle; the earlier barn, an English barn, had been used as a shelter for wounded Union soldiers, many of whom died in the fire that consumed it | =Constructed on the site of the battle-era barn, which was destroyed during the course of the battle. It had been used as a shelter for wounded Union soldiers, many of whom died in the ensuing fire that consumed the barn. Although the style of the {{bam|barn}} has changed since the battle from an English barn to a bank barn, its occupation of the historic barn site conveys the character of a building that served successively as cover and obstacle for both armies. | #s36-37
97 | hospitals | Joseph Sherfy barn (shelter for wounded) | bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Sherfy barn had been used as a shelter for wounded Union soldiers, many of whom died when it burned | It had been used as a shelter for wounded Union soldiers, many of whom died in the ensuing fire that consumed the barn. | #s36
98 | buildings | Henry Spangler barn (present building) | - | 1935 (reconstruction on pre-battle foundations) | stated | The present Henry Spangler barn was built in 1935 on the foundations of the pre-Civil War barn destroyed during the battle | #s1-3
98 | buildings | Henry Spangler house and barn, battle damage | - | 1863-07 | hedged | Although the barn was burned during the battle, damage to the Henry Spangler house was apparently limited to its contents, most of which were carried away for use by Confederate troops | =Although the {{bam|barn}} was burned during the battle, damage to the house was apparently limited to its contents (most of which were carried away for use by Confederate troops). | #s8
